PANTHEON MODULE SEED — VISUAL ANNOTATION / PAIN MAP ATELIER v1

Mission

Enable a person to DRAW DIRECTLY ON any uploaded image, not merely describe it in a separate note. Initially designed for mapping exact ankle pain and radiation paths on a phone; generalize the operator across medical symptom maps, shark-tooth sightings, field observations, diagram criticism, image forensics, and other spatially anchored semantics. Preserve the original image, mark coordinates, authorship, uncertainty, and exportability.

Artifact and location

Standalone working reference implementation: pain-map.html (self-contained HTML/CSS/JS; zero dependencies; offline; no data uploaded). It can be put at repo root as pain-map.html for easy GitHub Pages deployment. Private preloaded ankle images belong outside the public repository.

Hard invariants

1. Touch-first: one finger/stylus draws; two fingers pinch-zoom/pan without accidental marks; dedicated pan mode available.
2. Visible annotation layer over the unmodified image: freehand pen, arrow, circle, erase entire mark, undo, redo, clear.
3. Color/meaning key: red=primary pain; orange=radiation; blue=numbness; purple=scar/other. Allow independent legend customization later.
4. Coordinate preservation: strokes and shapes in normalized [0,1] image coordinates (u=left to right; v=top to bottom). Zoom/pan NEVER changes stored locations.
5. Original-resolution PNG export composites image+marks; interoperable JSON preserves strokes, colors, note, symptom type, timing, image dimensions, and provenance.
6. JSON import restores editable marks only against a photo of matching original pixel dimensions.
7. Local-only by default: no telemetry, servers, external libraries, or hidden uploads. Warn before publicly committing any personal/medical photo.
8. Never infer a definitive diagnosis or anatomy from a drawn mark. Separate observation, user-asserted interpretation, and clinical hypotheses.

Current schema

pantheon.visual-pain-map.v1:

• createdAt: ISO time
• image: name/width/height
• symptoms: sensation/timing/notes
• colorLegend: mapping of hex color to interpreted category
• coordinateSystem: normalized original image coordinates
• annotations: array of {kind:'pen'|'arrow'|'circle', color:'#...', size:number, points:[{u:number,v:number},...]}. The size is a fraction of original image width.

Transduction protocol

Observe: image pixels + user-drawn regions and paths + reported sensations.
Represent: coordinate-anchored marks, category legend, note, and image reference.
Orient: compare marks against relevant anatomy or domain ontology only as hypotheses.
Test: seek confirmatory evidence from clinician, source reports, repeat observation, or additional modalities.
Export: flattened human-readable picture + structured machine-rehydratable JSON.
Rehydrate: load original photo plus JSON, restore editability, allow refinement without losing provenance.

Forward design objectives (not yet implemented)

• Tap-to-place numbered callouts; editable labels and intensity gradients.
• Layered temporal pain maps (rest vs movement, day vs night) and comparison/delta engine.
• Segment/region tool with adjustable opacity instead of freehand-only marks.
• Import anatomical atlas overlays with side/orientation confirmation and clinical provenance.
• Version history/branching; reversible edits; annotated differences between visits.
• Accessible screen-reader descriptors and keyboard-first interactions.
• Optional AI interpretation layer showing competing structures and confidence, never silently converting a patient’s markup to a diagnosis.

Acceptance tests

A. iPhone portrait: load local photo, draw a red pain zone and orange path, pinch, pan, undo, redo, export full-resolution PNG.
B. Export JSON, reload the original image, import JSON, verify marks coincide within one original image pixel (coordinate round-trip).
C. Change zoom/pan and ensure identical exported coordinate positions.
D. App works offline with no build step and makes no network request.
E. Invalid or wrong-dimension JSON is rejected, not applied to an unrelated image.

One-shot implementation prompt for a future AI agent

“Implement or extend Pantheon Visual Annotation according to this seed. Preserve all hard invariants and schema compatibility. Make the smallest self-contained iPhone-operable artifact first. Test visible drawing, two-finger gestures, undo/redo, full-resolution PNG export, JSON round-trip, and no external requests. Treat annotations as user observations, not medical diagnoses. Give exact repository file paths and mobile GitHub commit instructions. Identify tested behavior separately from speculative future capability.”